WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.046
Here is a robot policy built on a video model.

00:00:03.046 --> 00:00:06.102
Does it watch the future play out before it moves the arm?

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:10.414
Hi, I'm Wystan!

00:00:10.414 --> 00:00:12.663
Praxis-1 is Runway's open-weight robot policy.

00:00:12.663 --> 00:00:16.411
Here's how a video model ends up driving an arm.

00:00:18.008 --> 00:00:18.581
Chapter one.

00:00:18.581 --> 00:00:20.301
Three ways to drive an arm.

00:00:24.525 --> 00:00:28.437
A robot policy maps what it sees to a short chunk of motion.

00:00:28.437 --> 00:00:29.339
Three families compete.

00:00:29.759 --> 00:00:32.072
A vision-language-action model starts from a language model.

00:00:32.072 --> 00:00:35.543
It knows what a comb is, not how it moves when pushed.

00:00:35.963 --> 00:00:38.497
A world model predicts the scene given an action.

00:00:38.497 --> 00:00:39.624
It is a simulator.

00:00:39.624 --> 00:00:40.750
It does not act.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:44.268
A world action model predicts frames and actions together.

00:00:44.268 --> 00:00:47.710
The bet: weights that imagine the scene learn the motion.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:49.087
And video is cheap.

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:51.529
Chapter two.

00:00:51.529 --> 00:00:53.236
Inside the fifty-four gigabytes.

00:00:58.008 --> 00:00:58.866
Runway's figure.

00:00:58.866 --> 00:01:04.015
A video backbone of fourteen encoder blocks, mostly mixture of experts: twenty-six

00:01:04.015 --> 00:01:07.876
point nine billion parameters, two point eight billion active.

00:01:08.296 --> 00:01:13.587
Below it, the action expert: sixteen layers, three hundred and fourteen million parameters.

00:01:13.587 --> 00:01:16.029
Every other layer is a cross-attention.

00:01:16.449 --> 00:01:19.879
Each reads one tapped encoder block, along these dotted lines.

00:01:21.342 --> 00:01:24.922
Each call takes an instruction, the latest frames from two cameras, and

00:01:24.922 --> 00:01:26.711
where the arm is right now.

00:01:27.131 --> 00:01:31.085
A mixture-of-experts video model starts denoising the next stretch of video.

00:01:31.505 --> 00:01:35.434
A small action expert taps eight of its layers, reading the future

00:01:35.434 --> 00:01:37.070
as the backbone imagines it.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:38.053
Nothing flows back.

00:01:38.473 --> 00:01:42.747
Out come twenty-four rows of motion: one point six seconds at fifteen hertz.

00:01:44.925 --> 00:01:48.138
Time is cut into chunks of one point six seconds.

00:01:48.138 --> 00:01:51.994
The exterior and wrist cameras sit side by side on one canvas.

00:01:52.414 --> 00:01:56.414
A frozen video autoencoder shrinks it sixteen times in each direction.

00:01:56.834 --> 00:02:00.933
Two by two patches make a hundred and eighty-two tokens per latent frame.

00:02:01.353 --> 00:02:05.462
Six latent frames: one thousand and ninety-two video tokens per chunk.

00:02:05.882 --> 00:02:07.793
The expert holds just fifteen tokens.

00:02:07.793 --> 00:02:10.977
The arm's motion is a rounding error in the sequence.

00:02:12.675 --> 00:02:13.586
Who reads what.

00:02:13.586 --> 00:02:15.408
Each row is one chunk's actions.

00:02:15.408 --> 00:02:19.356
They read the global tokens, and clean copies of the chunks before them.

00:02:19.776 --> 00:02:24.523
And the diagonal: the backbone's noised version of the very chunk being acted on.

00:02:24.523 --> 00:02:27.913
The actions read the future as the backbone imagines it.

00:02:30.008 --> 00:02:30.621
Chapter three.

00:02:30.621 --> 00:02:33.072
Two steps, and a video it never finishes.

00:02:36.475 --> 00:02:38.158
Here's the surprise in the code.

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:41.525
The next one point six seconds of motion are read off the

00:02:41.525 --> 00:02:44.611
backbone while its picture of them is still almost pure noise.

00:02:46.825 --> 00:02:50.066
Sampling walks time from one, pure noise, to zero, clean.

00:02:50.066 --> 00:02:53.954
The served config takes two steps and shifts the grid by twelve

00:02:53.954 --> 00:02:58.167
point five, so the middle call lands at zero point nine two six.

00:02:58.587 --> 00:03:01.307
Both the video and the actions start as pure noise.

00:03:01.727 --> 00:03:05.452
Step one runs the guided encoder and decoder, and moves the video

00:03:05.452 --> 00:03:07.936
latent seven point four percent of the way.

00:03:08.356 --> 00:03:10.371
Step two runs the encoder alone.

00:03:10.371 --> 00:03:14.065
The expert jumps the actions to clean in one move, and

00:03:14.065 --> 00:03:15.409
the decoder is skipped.

00:03:15.409 --> 00:03:20.111
On the article's toy, with a fifty-fifty context, the reach lands between the mugs.

00:03:20.531 --> 00:03:23.968
With eight steps and no shift, the action chunk gets clean enough,

00:03:23.968 --> 00:03:27.118
around a quarter of the way from the end, to resolve

00:03:27.118 --> 00:03:28.263
the choice by itself.

00:03:28.263 --> 00:03:29.982
The reach commits to one mug.

00:03:32.008 --> 00:03:33.984
So where do errors come from?

00:03:33.984 --> 00:03:37.607
Sampling its own video, the arm misses by five point seven

00:03:37.607 --> 00:03:38.924
one centimetres on average.

00:03:39.344 --> 00:03:42.848
Hand it the true future, noised to the same level, and the

00:03:42.848 --> 00:03:44.891
error drops to one point one four.

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:47.227
The mistakes live in which future it imagines.

00:03:49.342 --> 00:03:49.891
Chapter four.

00:03:49.891 --> 00:03:50.990
Does it hold up?

00:03:55.758 --> 00:04:00.052
On a hundred and twenty simulated tasks: forty-six point two percent.

00:04:00.052 --> 00:04:02.784
Fifth overall, first among world action models.

00:04:02.784 --> 00:04:03.955
From one run.

00:04:05.342 --> 00:04:09.439
The launch also set it against a frontier agent on a pen cap.

00:04:09.439 --> 00:04:12.276
Praxis-1 is done in seconds, the agent takes minutes.

00:04:12.696 --> 00:04:17.025
But that is a fine-tune on this robot against an agent running zero-shot.

00:04:17.025 --> 00:04:19.689
A gap in latency and cost, not capability.

00:04:21.192 --> 00:04:23.099
The article found three soft spots.

00:04:23.519 --> 00:04:27.668
Its most common failure is reaching for an object that resembles the target.

00:04:28.088 --> 00:04:32.206
Chunked execution means up to two point six nine seconds before a

00:04:32.206 --> 00:04:33.578
disturbance changes the motion.

00:04:33.998 --> 00:04:38.211
And every new robot is a full fine-tune of twenty-seven billion parameters.

00:04:39.808 --> 00:04:44.141
The robot moves on a future the model has barely started imagining.

00:04:44.141 --> 00:04:47.029
Better imagination is where the remaining errors live.

00:04:48.525 --> 00:04:52.852
A video model that imagines, an expert that reads it, two steps per chunk.

00:04:52.852 --> 00:04:53.779
Thanks for watching!

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.301
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:56.301 --> 00:04:56.901
I'm Wystan.

00:04:56.901 --> 00:04:57.201
Bye!
